{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB06723 - MONDO_6896",
  "verbalization": "Aluminum hydroxide is implicated in the pathogenesis of peptic esophagitis through its role in the treatment of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) and duodenal ulcers. The evidence suggests that aluminum hydroxide may exert a protective effect on the gastric and esophageal mucosa by neutralizing stomach acid, thereby reducing the risk of acid-induced damage. This mechanism is supported by its indication for treating duodenal ulcers and gastric ulcers, both of which are associated with peptic esophagitis. The indirect pathway through vonoprazan, a proton pump inhibitor, further underscores the potential of aluminum hydroxide to influence the gastrointestinal environment, potentially mitigating the risk of peptic esophagitis. The cumulative evidence, while not definitively establishing a direct causal relationship, supports the hypothesis that aluminum hydroxide may contribute to the prevention of peptic esophagitis through acid suppression and mucosal protection. The observed scores reflect the strength of the indirect pathways, but further mechanistic validation would be necessary to confirm the therapeutic relevance of this relationship.",
